What is middleware in Express and how do I use it?
Middleware in Express is essentially functions that are executed during the request-response cycle. They allow you to intercept requests, process them, and pass control to the next middleware in the stack or the final handler. Middleware is key for adding functionality like logging, authentication, error handling, or modifying request and response objects.

Types of Middleware
Built-in Middleware: Provided by Express (e.g., express.json(), express.static()).

Third-party Middleware: Installed via npm (e.g., body-parser, cookie-parser).

Custom Middleware: Functions you write yourself to handle specific tasks.

Anatomy of Middleware
Middleware functions take three arguments:

javascript
(req, res, next) => {
    // Middleware logic
    next(); // Pass control to the next middleware
}
The next() function is essential—it signals Express to move to the next middleware or route handler.

Example: Using Middleware
javascript
const express = require('express');
const app = express();

// Built-in middleware for parsing JSON
app.use(express.json());

// Custom middleware to log requests
app.use((req, res, next) => {
  console.log(`Request Method: ${req.method}, Request URL: ${req.url}`);
  next(); // Pass control to the next middleware
});

// Route handler
app.get('/', (req, res) => {
  res.send('Welcome to the homepage!');
});

// Error-handling middleware (optional)
app.use((err, req, res, next) => {
  console.error(err.stack);
  res.status(500).send('Something broke!');
});

// Start the server
app.listen(3000, () => {
  console.log('Server running on port 3000');
});
Where to Use Middleware
Middleware can be applied globally using app.use(), or locally to specific routes:

javascript
app.use(customMiddleware); // Global
app.get('/user', customMiddleware, userHandler); // Local
Common Middleware Applications:
Body parsing: Convert request bodies into usable objects (body-parser).

Session handling: Manage user sessions (express-session).

Logging: Track request details (morgan).

Authentication: Protect certain routes.

